  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants a forecast about whether an AI will contribute to solving one of the Millennium Prize Problems by various deadlines. The date they're forecasting from is 2025-04-29, so we're looking a few years into the future.\n\nFirst, I need to consider the problems left open. The Poincar\u00e9 conjecture was already solved, so the remaining ones are Birch/Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. These are all really tough and have been around for decades or longer. Solving any of them requires a significant breakthrough in mathematics.\n\nNow, the event in question is that a solution to at least one of these problems is publicly available, and AI provided an identifiable new argument or proof step. The correctness can be established later, but it needs to be a complete solution, not just partial progress.\n\nI need to estimate probabilities for each deadline. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050. The probabilities must be non-decreasing. Let's think about how likely AI contributions are in each time frame.\n\nBy 2026 (one year from the forecast date), I think it's very unlikely. AI has made strides in math, like in proof verification or generating conjectures, but a major breakthrough via AI would still be rare. Maybe a 0.05% chance? That seems low but possible.\n\nBy 2030, there's more time for AI to become more capable. But these problems are deep. Maybe the chance increases to 5% by then. Still, solving one of these is a monumental task, and even with AI, most progress would come from humans. Maybe 0.1% by 2030? Wait, maybe a bit higher if AI systems get really good. Let's say 1%.\n\nBy 2035, another five years, maybe AI becomes even more powerful. But again, these problems are expected to take decades, so maybe by 2035 there's a 3-5% chance. Hmm, but what if AI becomes AGI by then? Then solving might be more plausible. But I don't think we're there yet. Let me adjust. Maybe 2% by 2035.\n\nBy 2040, that's 15 years from the forecast date. If AGI develops, it could be possible. But if not, maybe it's still below 10%. Let's say 10% by 2040.\n\nBy 2050, 25 years out. If AI continues to advance, perhaps the chance is higher. Maybe 30% by 2050. But I need to consider that maybe these problems will never be solved at all, even by humans. So the probabilities should account for that possibility. But the question also says to account for the event never occurring, so even by 2050, the probability might be 50% or something. Wait, the instructions say to give cumulative probabilities for each deadline. So the probabilities should not exceed 1, but if the event cannot occur, they'd stay the same. However, the user wants cumulative, so each year should see a non-decreasing probability.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n2026: 0.001\n2030: 0.01\n2035: 0.05\n2040: 0.15\n2050: 0.4\n\nBut maybe that's too optimistic. Maybe the chances are even lower. Alternatively, maybe the rate increases more steeply if each year brings more AI prowess. Let me double-check the math.\n\nAnother thought: AI might help in generating proof strategies or searching for patterns, but actually proving one of these problems would require deep insight that AI might not have yet. So the probabilities should still be low. Maybe:\n\n2026: 0.001\n2030: 0.002\n2035: 0.01\n2040: 0.05\n2050: 0.2\n\nThat seems more reasonable. By 2050, even with AI, solving a Millennium Prize problem is a long shot. But the user says to account for the possibility that the event never occurs. So perhaps the probabilities are capped at, say, 0.2 by 2050. Hmm.\n\nI think I should adjust the numbers to be non-decreasing and realistic. Initially, very low, then a slight increase, but not too high. So final answer might be:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.001,\n  \"2030\": 0.002,\n  \"2035\": 0.005,\n  \"2040\": 0.01,\n  \"2050\": 0.02\n}\n\nThat is, each decade the chance doubles, but even by 2050, it's only 2%. But maybe that's too conservative? Maybe optimistic.\n\nAlternatively, considering that AI might be used in collaboration with humans to find proofs, maybe the probabilities should increase more rapidly. For example, 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, 5%, 10% for the respective dates. But that's more optimistic.\n\nBut which is more accurate? Since the problems are extremely challenging, even with AI, I think the probabilities should remain low. Let's say 0.001 (0.1%), then 0.005, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1 for the deadlines. That's non-decreasing and cumulative. So:\n\n2026: 0.001\n2030: 0.005\n2035: 0.01\n2040: 0.05\n2050: 0.1\n\nThat seems plausible. A 10% chance by 2050 that AI contributed to solving a problem. The longer the time, the higher the chance. But since the problems are hard, even with AI, it's not that high. So the final probabilities would be as above.\n\nSo the JSON object would be:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.001,\n  \"2030\": 0.005,\n  \"2035\": 0.01,\n  \"2040\": 0.05,\n  \"2050\": 0.1\n}\n\nJust ensuring that the probabilities are non-decreasing and between 0 and 1.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.001, \"2030\": 0.005, \"2035\": 0.01, \"2040\": 0.05, \"2050\": 0.1}",
